### Float32 semantics (§1)

The constructor stores `Math.cos(i * 0.017453292519943295)` into a `Float32Array`. In Java, `(float)Math.cos(...)` rounds a `double` to `float32` with `round-to-nearest-even`. Assigning a JS `double` into a `Float32Array` slot uses the same rounding mode (IEEE 754). The values will be bit-identical.

`getsin()` and `getcos()` read from `Float32Array` — the returned JS number is the float32 value widened to double, which is the same number Java's `float` widens to `double`. **No `fr()` call is needed inside these methods.** Callers that use the returned value in arithmetic **must** wrap each binary op in `fr()` (§1 of the spec, already documented in the file header).

